1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
A glass of milk sits on a warm

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:02,640
bench.

3
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,320
At first it's fine.

4
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,000
Within a day or two it smells sour.

5
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,280
But that milk started with only a few

6
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,400
bacteria, and a bacterium
has no partner to

7
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:13,040
join with.

8
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:16,239
So how does one cell make another cell?

9
00:00:16,281 --> 00:00:18,480
And once it can, how does that become

10
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:19,280
a crowd?

11
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,660
In the first video of this series you

12
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,100
saw this as an example of copying.

13
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:27,460
Now let's see how it works, step by

14
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:28,260
step.

15
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:30,100
Here is one bacterium.

16
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,980
Its DNA is a single loop, lying in

17
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:33,540
the cell.

18
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:36,420
Suppose the cell simply pinched in half.

19
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:38,740
One half would get the loop, and the

20
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:40,260
other half would get nothing.

21
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:43,220
The DNA holds
the instructions a cell needs

22
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:45,540
to grow and work, so a cell with

23
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:46,740
none can't.

24
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:48,980
So the first step is to copy the

25
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:52,660
DNA, and now there
are two identical loops.

26
00:00:53,867 --> 00:00:56,186
The cell grows longer, and the two loops

27
00:00:56,267 --> 00:00:58,666
move apart, one towards each end.

28
00:00:59,307 --> 00:01:01,546
Then a new wall grows across the middle

29
00:01:01,627 --> 00:01:03,386
and pinches the cell in two.

30
00:01:04,027 --> 00:01:06,346
Each new cell has its own loop, so

31
00:01:06,427 --> 00:01:08,346
each is a complete bacterium.

32
00:01:08,907 --> 00:01:10,826
This is called binary fission.

33
00:01:11,307 --> 00:01:14,426
Binary means two,
and fission means splitting.

34
00:01:14,907 --> 00:01:18,746
It's asexual: one parent,
nothing joins, and the

35
00:01:18,827 --> 00:01:21,146
two new cells are clones of the parent,

36
00:01:21,227 --> 00:01:23,146
apart from rare copying errors.

37
00:01:24,367 --> 00:01:26,166
Now, how fast?

38
00:01:26,447 --> 00:01:29,246
In warm conditions
with plenty of food, some

39
00:01:29,287 --> 00:01:31,806
bacteria can divide as often as every 20

40
00:01:31,967 --> 00:01:32,726
minutes.

41
00:01:33,007 --> 00:01:34,526
Follow one cell.

42
00:01:34,687 --> 00:01:36,846
After 20 minutes there are two.

43
00:01:36,927 --> 00:01:38,606
Each of those splits in the next 20

44
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:41,406
minutes, so there are four,
then eight, then

45
00:01:41,567 --> 00:01:42,606
sixteen.

46
00:01:42,687 --> 00:01:45,166
Every time, all the cells split, so the

47
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:47,086
whole crowd doubles.

48
00:01:47,247 --> 00:01:49,966
So how many cells after 3 hours?

49
00:01:50,087 --> 00:01:52,766
Three hours is 180 minutes.

50
00:01:52,847 --> 00:01:55,566
Each doubling takes 20
minutes, so we count

51
00:01:55,647 --> 00:01:59,646
how many 20-minute steps
fit into 180 minutes.

52
00:01:59,727 --> 00:02:03,086
180 divided by 20 is 9, so that's

53
00:02:03,167 --> 00:02:04,606
nine doublings.

54
00:02:04,687 --> 00:02:07,646
Each doubling multiplies
the crowd by 2, so

55
00:02:07,727 --> 00:02:11,246
nine doublings means nine
2s multiplied together, 2

56
00:02:11,287 --> 00:02:15,006
to the power 9, which is 512 cells.

57
00:02:15,127 --> 00:02:17,246
Keep going for 10 hours and that's thirty

58
00:02:17,287 --> 00:02:20,686
doublings, about a billion
cells, but only if

59
00:02:20,767 --> 00:02:22,486
the food never runs out.

60
00:02:22,527 --> 00:02:24,686
In real milk, the acid and other waste

61
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:27,566
build up, so growth slows and stops.

62
00:02:28,767 --> 00:02:30,846
Yeast is a fungus that lives as a

63
00:02:31,007 --> 00:02:32,206
single cell.

64
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:34,926
Baker's yeast, the kind
used to make bread,

65
00:02:35,007 --> 00:02:38,566
usually reproduces by budding
when sugar is plentiful.

66
00:02:38,847 --> 00:02:41,566
A small bump, called a bud, grows out

67
00:02:41,647 --> 00:02:43,166
from the side of the parent cell.

68
00:02:43,807 --> 00:02:46,206
In a yeast cell the DNA is inside

69
00:02:46,247 --> 00:02:49,086
a nucleus, a small
compartment that holds it.

70
00:02:49,727 --> 00:02:52,446
The yeast copies its DNA, and one copy

71
00:02:52,607 --> 00:02:53,646
moves into the bud.

72
00:02:54,287 --> 00:02:56,686
The bud grows bigger,
using materials from the

73
00:02:56,767 --> 00:02:57,326
parent.

74
00:02:57,887 --> 00:03:00,686
Then a wall forms across the narrow neck,

75
00:03:00,727 --> 00:03:02,686
and the bud breaks away as a new

76
00:03:02,767 --> 00:03:04,846
cell, a clone of its parent.

77
00:03:05,487 --> 00:03:07,646
The steps are the same as in fission:

78
00:03:07,727 --> 00:03:09,726
copy, share, split.

79
00:03:10,207 --> 00:03:11,606
But there's one difference.

80
00:03:11,887 --> 00:03:15,086
In fission, the parent
becomes two new cells.

81
00:03:15,487 --> 00:03:18,286
In budding, the parent carries on, and a

82
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:19,886
new cell grows from it.

83
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:23,580
Protists are a mixed
group of living things

84
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,780
that are not animals, plants or fungi, and

85
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:28,540
many of them live in pond water.

86
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:30,700
Some are single cells.

87
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,980
An amoeba is a shapeless cell that crawls

88
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:34,540
along.

89
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:38,140
It copies its DNA,
inside its nucleus, then

90
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:40,220
pulls apart into two amoebae.

91
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:42,220
That's binary fission again.

92
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:45,820
A paramecium is a slipper-shaped
cell covered in

93
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:46,860
tiny hairs.

94
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:49,980
It copies its DNA, then divides across the

95
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,460
middle, so each new cell gets its own

96
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:53,100
copy.

97
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,260
And a few protists
reproduce by budding, such

98
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:00,220
as the stalked Acineta,
which releases a small

99
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:01,020
swimming bud.

100
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,500
So the same two methods turn up in

101
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:04,940
very different organisms.

102
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,740
Copy, share, split.

103
00:04:08,967 --> 00:04:10,846
One more thing about bacteria.

104
00:04:11,127 --> 00:04:14,486
Sometimes two bacteria
touch, and a thin bridge

105
00:04:14,567 --> 00:04:16,166
forms between them.

106
00:04:16,247 --> 00:04:18,246
One cell passes a copy of some of

107
00:04:18,327 --> 00:04:20,286
its DNA across to the other.

108
00:04:20,567 --> 00:04:22,326
This is called conjugation.

109
00:04:22,967 --> 00:04:24,246
Now count the cells.

110
00:04:24,647 --> 00:04:26,086
Before, there were two.

111
00:04:26,567 --> 00:04:28,166
After, there are still two.

112
00:04:28,647 --> 00:04:31,526
Nothing divided, so no new cell was made.

113
00:04:32,167 --> 00:04:35,286
For a single-celled organism,
making a new individual

114
00:04:35,447 --> 00:04:36,966
means making a new cell.

115
00:04:37,447 --> 00:04:39,606
So conjugation isn't reproduction.

116
00:04:40,007 --> 00:04:42,326
It changes what the cells carry, so it

117
00:04:42,407 --> 00:04:44,726
can spread a feature through a crowd, such

118
00:04:44,807 --> 00:04:47,846
as resistance to an antibiotic,
which means surviving

119
00:04:47,927 --> 00:04:50,246
a drug that kills bacteria or stops them

120
00:04:50,327 --> 00:04:51,086
growing.

121
00:04:51,447 --> 00:04:53,326
Fission makes more cells.

122
00:04:53,527 --> 00:04:56,566
Conjugation changes
cells that already exist.

123
00:04:57,207 --> 00:05:00,006
Fast copying makes lots
of cells, but copies

124
00:05:00,087 --> 00:05:02,566
of one cell are alike, apart from rare

125
00:05:02,647 --> 00:05:03,286
errors.

126
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,300
Put the three side by side.

127
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,700
A bacterium, a yeast and a paramecium each

128
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:13,140
copy their DNA, share
the copies and split,

129
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:15,780
and when they reproduce this way no cell

130
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:16,820
joins another.

131
00:05:18,034 --> 00:05:18,873
Your turn.

132
00:05:19,074 --> 00:05:20,433
Three situations.

133
00:05:20,674 --> 00:05:24,033
One: a scientist watches
a bacterium split into

134
00:05:24,194 --> 00:05:25,073
two cells.

135
00:05:25,634 --> 00:05:27,953
One new cell has one loop of DNA,

136
00:05:28,194 --> 00:05:29,633
and the other has none.

137
00:05:30,274 --> 00:05:33,633
Which step went wrong
first, copy, share or

138
00:05:33,714 --> 00:05:36,033
split, and what will the cell with no

139
00:05:36,114 --> 00:05:37,953
DNA be unable to do?

140
00:05:38,834 --> 00:05:41,953
Two: two bacteria lie side by side.

141
00:05:42,354 --> 00:05:44,993
A thin bridge forms between them, and one

142
00:05:45,154 --> 00:05:46,673
passes a copy of a gene to the

143
00:05:46,754 --> 00:05:47,073
other.

144
00:05:47,634 --> 00:05:49,793
Then the bridge breaks and they separate.

145
00:05:50,274 --> 00:05:51,393
Is this reproduction?

146
00:05:51,874 --> 00:05:54,113
What changed, and what stayed the same?

147
00:05:54,914 --> 00:05:58,833
Three: a different bacterium
divides every 40 minutes.

148
00:05:59,314 --> 00:06:01,393
One cell goes into a flask with plenty

149
00:06:01,474 --> 00:06:02,673
of food and space.

150
00:06:03,154 --> 00:06:06,113
Every cell divides on time, and none die.

151
00:06:06,674 --> 00:06:08,993
How many cells are there after 2 hours?

152
00:06:09,634 --> 00:06:11,473
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

153
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:15,020
One, the copy step did not happen, so

154
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:16,860
there was only one loop to give out.

155
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:20,300
Sharing needs two loops,
so copying is where

156
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:21,020
it went wrong.

157
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:24,140
A cell with no DNA has no instructions

158
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:26,700
to follow, so it can't grow or work

159
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:27,380
properly.

160
00:07:27,660 --> 00:07:30,380
If you said share,
remember that sharing needs

161
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:31,820
two loops to hand out.

162
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:33,740
And if you said both cells would be

163
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:36,860
fine because they split
evenly, size isn't the

164
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:37,260
point.

165
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:39,660
A cell can look healthy and still hold

166
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:40,780
no instructions.

167
00:07:41,420 --> 00:07:42,700
Two, no.

168
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:44,060
Count the cells.

169
00:07:44,460 --> 00:07:47,020
Before, there were two,
and after, there are

170
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:47,740
still two.

171
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:50,540
A copy of a gene moved, so one

172
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:52,300
cell now carries something new.

173
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:53,980
That is what changed.

174
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:56,540
The number of cells stayed the same, and

175
00:07:56,700 --> 00:07:58,860
copy and share happened, but there was no

176
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:01,620
split, so no new cell was made.

177
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,740
If you said yes because a gene was

178
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:06,380
passed on, remember that passing on a gene

179
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:08,260
changes what a cell carries.

180
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:09,820
It doesn't add a cell.

181
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:11,860
Three, eight.

182
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:16,140
Two hours is 120 minutes, and 120 divided

183
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:18,220
by 40 is 3 doublings.

184
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:21,900
One cell becomes 2, then 4, then 8.

185
00:08:22,380 --> 00:08:24,620
If you said 6, you may have multiplied

186
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:25,980
the 3 by 2.

187
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:28,780
If you said 9, you squared the 3.

188
00:08:29,340 --> 00:08:31,820
If you said 3, you counted the doublings

189
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:33,820
but did not double the crowd.

190
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:36,220
Each doubling doubles the whole crowd.

191
00:08:37,434 --> 00:08:39,673
So, keep hold of one pattern.

192
00:08:40,074 --> 00:08:42,913
Copy the DNA first, then split.

193
00:08:43,194 --> 00:08:45,033
Copy, share, split.

194
00:08:45,674 --> 00:08:48,953
In fission, the parent
becomes two new cells.

195
00:08:49,194 --> 00:08:51,433
In budding, a new cell grows from the

196
00:08:51,514 --> 00:08:51,993
parent.

197
00:08:52,634 --> 00:08:55,673
Conjugation copies
and shares DNA too, but it

198
00:08:55,834 --> 00:08:58,473
never splits, so it makes no new cell.

199
00:08:59,114 --> 00:09:00,713
Fission makes more cells.

200
00:09:01,114 --> 00:09:04,073
Conjugation changes
cells that already exist.

201
00:09:04,874 --> 00:09:07,593
Next: what happens after a mammal's egg is

202
00:09:07,674 --> 00:09:08,553
fertilised.

203
00:09:09,767 --> 00:09:11,206
Here are the key notes.

204
00:09:11,447 --> 00:09:13,286
Pause the video if you need more time

205
00:09:13,367 --> 00:09:14,166
to copy them.
